{com}{sf}{ul off}{txt}{hline 110}
{res}Table 1 | Supermarket access and women's food shopping and cooking time
{txt}{hline 110}
Study: Supermarkets benefit women by reducing time spent on unpaid housework without lowering nutritional quality.
Data: China Health and Nutrition Survey, 2004, 2006, 2009 and 2011; unweighted panel analysis.
Treatment: supermarket access within 5 km. Always-treated communities are excluded.
Controls: age, age squared, dietary knowledge, household size, ownership of a car, motorcycle,
refrigerator, microwave and pressure cooker, traditional market development, and local economic activity.
Fixed effects: individual and province by survey period.
Standard errors: clustered by community (women-only community-sex identifier).
All numerical results below are recomputed from the supplied analysis data in Stata 18 MP.
N reports observations used by the estimator; model input counts are shown separately.
Significance: *** p<0.01; ** p<0.05; * p<0.10. Confidence level: 95%.
Panel A: CS-DID regression adjustment with never-treated controls; overall ATET.
Panels B-C: two-stage DID with controls in the first stage and individual fixed effects.
Panel B estimates the effect per additional supermarket; Panel C uses 0 as the reference category.
Columns: (1) log shopping minutes; (2) shopping minutes; (3) log cooking minutes; (4) cooking minutes.
Zero shopping time is retained in level specifications and has no defined logarithm.

{res}Sample size note for Panel A
{txt}Complete-case observations in columns (1)-(4): 4,528; 5,160; 5,098; 5,098.
CS-DID estimation observations in columns (1)-(4): 4,394; 5,093; 5,031; 5,031.
The complete-case counts are also reproduced by reg and xtreg with individual fixed effects.
CS-DID identifies treatment cohorts within each outcome-specific, complete-case individual panel.
Individuals with no observed untreated period are classified as Always treated and excluded.
This rule excludes 134 observations in column (1), and 67 in each of columns (2)-(4).
For log shopping time, 70 excluded observations belong to 47 women whose earlier untreated
shopping times were all zero: taking logarithms makes those earlier outcomes missing.
The other 64 excluded log-shopping observations have no untreated record in the supplied data.
In columns (2)-(4), the 67 excluded observations belong to 50 women with no untreated record.
Single-observation individuals are not excluded as a general rule: CS-DID retains 716 such
observations in column (1), 700 in column (2), and 646 in each of columns (3)-(4).
Counts refer to individual-wave observations. Each panel reports its estimator-specific N below.

